      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.stselectronicrecyclinginc.com/bakersfield-ca#faqpage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What certifications do you hold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS follows NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1 sanitization guidance and physical destruction methods aligned to DoD 5220.22-M. Files also support HIPAA reviews, ISO 27001 programs, and SOC 2 customer audit requests across regulated Bakersfield teams. Healthcare clients such as Kern Medical and Adventist Health Bakersfield typically receive Business Associate Agreements and certificates of destruction. Corporate IT Directors use serial-level documentation for internal audits and insurer reviews. Equipment collected in Bakersfield is processed with chain of custody from intake through final disposition, and reports typically return within 48 hours."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What areas do you serve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS provides scheduled electronics pickup across Bakersfield, Oildale, Rosedale, East Bakersfield, Downtown, and greater Kern County. Qualifying commercial volumes typically receive free pickup, and most organizations can book same-week routing. Coverage also includes Lamont, Arvin, Shafter, Wasco, Delano, McFarland, Tehachapi, Taft, and Ridgecrest. Routes typically cover downtown offices, Rosedale campuses, and warehouse docks in one window. Organizations searching for electronics recycling near me throughout Bakersfield find STS provides scheduled pickup in Oildale, Rosedale, and all Kern County locations."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is pickup really free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For qualifying commercial volumes, scheduled pickup is free across Bakersfield and the greater Kern County area for business sites. Most organizations typically receive a no-obligation quote and same-week scheduling once STS confirms asset types, counts, and site access. Complimentary pickup applies to offices, clinics, and warehouse docks. There are no hidden fees on qualifying commercial pickups. Volume discounts are available when a project includes multiple Kern County sites or large pallets."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you provide audit documentation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Each completed Bakersfield pickup typically includes chain of custody records plus a Certificate of Destruction for retired media. Serialized reports help compliance officers attach those files to internal audits, insurers, and outside regulator reviews. Corporate IT Directors typically expect detailed certificates of destruction for audit reviews, included in every STS service engagement. Accurate reporting typically returns within 48 hours of processing and helps finance, IT, and compliance teams close the asset record. Serial numbers, methods, and downstream tracking stay with the file."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens to my equipment after pickup?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "After pickup from Bakersfield, every retired asset is logged at intake, then data-bearing media is sanitized or physically destroyed before recovery. Remaining commodities then move through documented downstream processors under a zero-landfill policy. Overwrite, degaussing, or physical destruction that exceeds NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1 guidance is used when media stored sensitive data. NSA-approved degaussers are available when policy calls for magnetic erasure. You receive chain of custody files and a Certificate of Destruction, typically within 48 hours."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you handle large-scale ITAD projects?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. District and campus refreshes, including seasonal projects at Kern High School District, often need staged pickup windows. The 200,000 sq ft processing operation also handles office sweeps, warehouse pallets, and data center decommissions for Bakersfield clients. California State University, Bakersfield and energy operators such as Chevron generate enterprise volumes that fit the same workflow. Complimentary pickup applies to qualifying volumes. STS confirms asset types, site access, and timing on a no-obligation call before same-week scheduling for most Kern County organizations."
          }
        }
      ]
